1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,540
Loopback Transformer:

2
00:00:01,540 --> 00:00:05,290
Towards a Unified View of
Residual Looped Transformers.

3
00:00:06,510 --> 00:00:09,200
Linear attention is a memory over tokens:

4
00:00:09,810 --> 00:00:14,120
each token writes its key and value
into a state that is passed along,

5
00:00:14,330 --> 00:00:16,280
and later tokens read from it.

6
00:00:16,690 --> 00:00:19,340
The residual stream does
the same over depth.

7
00:00:19,990 --> 00:00:22,620
Each block writes its
update into the stream.

8
00:00:23,090 --> 00:00:24,710
The stream carries it forward,

9
00:00:24,710 --> 00:00:26,880
and later blocks read from it.

10
00:00:27,230 --> 00:00:30,990
Unrolled, every read is a
weighted sum of what was written;

11
00:00:30,990 --> 00:00:35,060
plain residual addition
fixes every weight at one.

12
00:00:36,380 --> 00:00:37,230
In this view,

13
00:00:37,320 --> 00:00:39,990
every residual rule sets three things:

14
00:00:40,340 --> 00:00:41,750
the strength of the input,

15
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:43,480
the total weight of the updates,

16
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:45,210
and how that weight is shared.

17
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:49,050
Residual addition gives
every update a weight of one,

18
00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:51,690
so the total grows with every pass.

19
00:00:52,660 --> 00:00:54,950
Hyper-Connections give
each stream one weight,

20
00:00:55,040 --> 00:00:57,990
so the input and the
updates in it move together.

21
00:00:58,740 --> 00:01:02,970
Attention Residuals weigh the input
and the updates with one softmax,

22
00:01:03,240 --> 00:01:05,160
so their weights add up to one:

23
00:01:05,160 --> 00:01:08,830
more weight on the updates
leaves less for the input.

24
00:01:10,090 --> 00:01:12,140
Loopback sets the three separately.

25
00:01:12,750 --> 00:01:15,460
The input keeps weight
one on its own path.

26
00:01:15,950 --> 00:01:17,610
A decay bounds the total,

27
00:01:17,610 --> 00:01:20,820
and attention shares it
across the stored updates.

28
00:01:22,270 --> 00:01:25,300
Here are our training
curves at all five scales.

29
00:01:26,050 --> 00:01:31,060
Loopback's loss is lower from early
in training all the way to the end.

30
00:01:32,420 --> 00:01:35,990
From 191M to 3B parameters,

31
00:01:36,340 --> 00:01:39,270
Loopback gets lower loss than
the standard Transformer,

32
00:01:39,460 --> 00:01:42,330
with 40–75% of its parameters.

33
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:48,740
It has the lowest loss and the highest
benchmark average of all nine designs,

34
00:01:48,740 --> 00:01:51,450
at all three sizes we tested.

35
00:01:53,250 --> 00:01:57,120
We also held the parameter count
fixed and traded width for depth.

36
00:01:57,590 --> 00:01:59,720
As the model gets deeper and narrower,

37
00:02:00,070 --> 00:02:02,400
the standard Transformer loses ground,

38
00:02:02,550 --> 00:02:05,120
while Loopback's margin
grows at both scales.

39
00:02:06,550 --> 00:02:07,500
That's Loopback.

40
00:02:07,790 --> 00:02:08,740
Fewer parameters,

41
00:02:08,890 --> 00:02:09,580
lower loss.

42
00:02:10,010 --> 00:02:12,320
The paper and code are
on our project page.

